king in New York you  know way back in the 70s and the guy  wanted to fly to Europe and in part of  his discussions with the negotiators he  said I want next time you come out here  bring me a hot cup of coffee with cream  and two  Sugar's you know an hour later the  coffee comes out then it's cold and  there's no sugar and there's no cream  and the guy surrenders about 20 minutes  later and they said well what was it  that made you surrendered he said well I  figured if I couldn't get a decent cup  of coffee this other stuff wasn't gonna  work out you know and it and it's sort  of a humorous way to look at kind of how  the expectations got lowered and the  reality set in that hey you're not the  Big Kahuna you don't just say you want  something and it's gonna happen you got  to go through us now those incidents  that I just described it's only about  ten percent really of what we do most  police officers will never work a  legitimate hostage situation what  they're working 90 percent is  emotionally driven situations where the  victim not really a hostage the victim  and the person holding them you know  have a pre-existing relationship it's an  employee employer it's a  boyfriend/girlfriend husband/wife you  know neighbor neighbor whatever it might  be they have a history that is tense  problematic and confrontational that has  erupted into this event and those are  the cases where the person really  doesn't know how to get out of what they  got into they just aren't expressing  their emotions and our job in those  cases is not so much to make a deal but  to lower their emotional levels get them  to a point where they're thinking a  little more clearly a little more  rationally and realizing that anything  violent they they do is just  self-defeating and self-destructive and  we're pretty successful at those as well  but I will tell you when there is loss  of life it's in those situations not a  hostage situation  interesting  you  [Music]
 

